(FIL-52, FIL-53, FIL-54, FIL-55: the four rows `Read line` leaves the
 rest of the section entirely untouched - LANGUAGE.md:3994-3998. One
 file, two lines: the first ends in a newline, the second does NOT
 (the final line of the file), which puts both halves of "up to `\n` or
 EOF" on trial in one probe.

 First `Read line` gets the first line: size == line length + 1 proves
 the trailing newline is RETAINED (FIL-53). Second `Read line`, into the
 SAME buffer with no re-seed, gets the second line: size == that line's
 own length PROVES TWO CLAIMS AT ONCE, the same reasoning FIL-46's own
 leaf above already uses for a different pair - an appending bug would
 inflate this size well past the second line's length (FIL-55, replace
 not append), and the line came back WHOLE with no newline appended
 (FIL-52, final line with no trailing `\n` reads exactly as written). A
 third `Read line`, past EOF, must return an empty buffer and must NOT
 raise the error flag (FIL-54) - guarded by the same stale-flag drain
 'gen leaf buffer fixed overflow' (gen_buffers.vox) established, so a
 pending flag from an earlier, unrelated leaf never masquerades as this
 read's own outcome. Probes: docs/ledger/probes/files/FIL-52.vox,
 FIL-53.vox, FIL-54.vox.)

